 @davidbrennan5  the bulk of the volume of the PC market has GPUs 8GB or less & VRAM isn't getting any cheaper so that's unlikely to change any time soon. The games where this is a problem are the PS5-native games designed around 16GB that were never initially designed to scale down like that. Post-release we're seeing more games getting optimisations to caching & texture pooling, I don't see any developers who're designing games for Series S having this problem. It's the result of a cost:benefit calculation done where we're seeing games built from the ground up for the new consoles for the first time & publishers weighing up the PC platform optimisations vs just getting the sales now & fixing performance later.

There's still the other issue of stutter caused by SSD loading on PC which on the consoles is handled by dedicated hardware streaming directly into VRAM instead of processor-side into system memory first & even the most powerful CPUs on the market aren't able to eliminate this. If PC hardware doesn't address this directly as part of the platform of the Zen 5 / Core 15 series then developers will need to address this discrepancy in a more systemic way.

Though it might be ultimately that PC and Console gaming fork off from each other in a similar way to what they did pre-PS4/Xbone when the foundational hardware was a completely different non-x86 architecture. I doubt it though.

